Serve validated static raster previews with measured MIME and nosniff; other original/unknown content uses attachment and sanitized filename plus Content-Security-Policy:sandbox. Do not serve user SVG/HTML as active app-origin content. Referrer-Policy:no-referrer; no sensitive response caching. Same-origin browser GET fetches can omit Origin; the authenticated client uses X-App-Client:LP-1 and Sec-Fetch-Site:same-origin under the narrow read exception above. Use fetch-based SSE so the client marker is available. Never attempt to set the browser-controlled Origin header or waive checks for generic absent-Origin requests. Browser behavior and proxy details require Q26 qualification; unsupported browser behavior must not silently disable checks.

### 20.3 Egress and transfer profile EF-1

Separate queue transport from asset retrieval. Only authenticated queue calls to exact https://queue.fal.run:443 for the selected allowlisted endpoint/request identity can receive Authorization: Key from CredentialProvider; no redirects. The dedicated fal upload adapter may receive a credential only for exact documented auth/API endpoints recorded in its sealed Q09 profile; never for a returned asset URL. Queue-returned URLs must match the pinned queue origin and request identity, otherwise quarantine for protocol reconciliation. No generic fal.run POST or model stream is enabled by this policy.

Credential-free media fetches use a versioned server-owned exact-host list in the selected Q09 storage profile, with only HTTPS port 443. No wildcard suffix list, arbitrary host, browser-supplied URL, userinfo, fragment, ambient cookie, Authorization, client certificate, .netrc or environment proxy inheritance. The initial list is empty until the documented fal/CDN endpoint hosts are sealed; this is a qualification gate, not permission to guess a current CDN hostname. Signed query credentials received from the provider remain confined to backend transport evidence. Media redirects are rejected (zero hops); a newly documented host/redirect needs a profile revision and fixture review before use, never credential forwarding.

Resolve A/AAAA at connection time, reject the whole answer set if any address is loopback, private, link-local, multicast, unspecified or reserved, including IPv4-mapped IPv6 and noncanonical numeric forms. Pin a validated address to the actual socket, verify peer address and TLS hostname/certificate for the original host, and revalidate each new connection/resume; do not validate one DNS answer then reconnect through a new unchecked resolution. Deny proxy bypass. Test-only provider emulation is a distinct dependency-injected transport with fixed loopback fixture origins and sentinel credentials; production builds/config reject the emulator flag/profile and cannot weaken EF-1.

Every transfer owns a R31 disk reservation and purpose envelope, counts actual streamed bytes even without Content-Length, and checks declared versus actual length/hash. A known size cannot exceed reserved bytes; unknown size pauses before consuming the reservation and acquires more under R31 or fails visibly, preserving partial evidence. No new global file cap is implied: larger full closures and opaque returned prompts stream under R38/R39; training keeps its four caps. Use ≤1MiB IO chunks inside the existing 64MiB copy/text workspaces, with codecs/tensors inside their existing caps. Connection deadline 10 seconds; no-progress read deadline 30 seconds. Each timeout aborts that connection and reports interrupted retrieval, never provider cancellation. There is no absolute job-duration timeout; resumable bounded transfers can continue while bytes make progress and reservations remain valid. Status/read/transfer retry uses existing bounded backoff and observed Retry-After; each attempt rechecks identity, storage and session. Do not auto-retry a paid submission. New operational connection limits are distinct from unchanged end-to-end budget ceilings and are accounted inside the parent clocks.

### 20.4 Portable format and upload profile PF-1

.ideogram-project formatVersion:1 is ZIP64-capable ZIP with STORE entries (no compression, encryption, multi-volume archives or executable content). This explicitly chooses the formerly proposed container; STORE removes decompression expansion from the initial profile without limiting closure size. Reject other compression methods as unsupported profile, never auto-convert untrusted files. UTF-8 entry names are exact canonical relative ASCII paths: manifest.json, records/<decimal>.jsonl and objects/<64 lowercase hex sha256>. No absolute/drive/UNC paths, backslashes, percent-escaped names, dot/dot-dot/empty segments, duplicate names, case-fold collisions, symlink/hardlink/device entries or overlapping byte ranges. Directory entries are unnecessary and rejected. ZIP local headers and central directory must agree on name/method/sizes/CRC; validate ZIP64 offsets/lengths without narrowing to 32-bit. The caller never supplies a destination path.

```ts
type PortableSegment = {
  path: string; sha256: string; bytes: string;
  kind: "index" | "events" | "records"; recordCount: string;
};
type PortableRootRef = { kind: string; logicalId: string; recordHash: string };
type PortableManifestV1 = {
  formatVersion: 1; documentSchema: 2; sourceNamespace: string;
  capturedHighWater: Seq; complete: boolean;
  rootRefs: readonly PortableRootRef[]; segments: readonly PortableSegment[];
};
type PortableRecordV1 =
  | { schemaVersion: 1; kind: "index"; segments: readonly PortableSegment[] }
  | { schemaVersion: 1; kind: "event"; event: DomainEvent<unknown> }
  | { schemaVersion: 1; kind: "object"; sha256: string; bytes: string;
      mediaType: string; path: string }
  | { schemaVersion: 1; kind: "entity"; logicalId: string; entityType: string;
      payloadVersion: number; payloadRef: BlobRef; dependencies: readonly PortableRootRef[] };
```

Manifest is {formatVersion:1,documentSchema:2,sourceNamespace,capturedHighWater,complete,rootRefs,segments}; each segment descriptor is {path,sha256,bytes,kind,recordCount}. Manifest pages and individual descriptor/event records obey existing R39 128MiB segment and R19/§5 event envelopes; larger indexes are segmented, not truncated. A small manifest root points to further descriptor segments rather than requiring all descriptors resident. JSONL records are UTF-8 LP-1 canonical objects with one record per line and schema/payload versions. Event sequence and transaction boundaries are retained; closure maps include immutable hashes, lengths, logical IDs, dependencies and artifact class. Object descriptors and raw SHA-256 hex filenames agree; BlobRef hashes retain their algorithm-qualified form. Entity types are limited to the existing document/layer/text/layout/font/composition/history/checkpoint/draft/job/candidate/dataset/adapter schemas and TP-1 portable-provider, never backend-transport; unknown versions remain inspection-only. Index segments form an acyclic graph; event segments preserve exact original event objects and transaction boundaries. Objects contain the exact owned bytes. Source namespace IDs remap into a fresh local namespace with a persisted mapping; original bytes/identity remain provenance. Imported jobs are observation-only. Unknown future format/version opens inspection/recovery without executing or publishing an editable namespace; never coerce to v1.

Validation order is normative: (1) authenticated ownership and purpose/declared length plus R31 reservation; (2) stream to isolated owner-only staging with hash/length checks; (3) bounded ZIP directory/header/entry-type/path/collision/range validation; (4) stream STORE entries, CRC and SHA-256, enforcing aggregate bytes equal the declared reservation and descriptors (there is no compression ratio >1 to accept); (5) validate versioned manifest/JSONL without duplicate keys, references, complete transaction sequences and full transitive closure; (6) validate typed assets/codecs/fonts/adapters under their sealed profiles without executing archive code; (7) display preview including missing/restricted fonts, scope/size and namespace mapping; (8) one writer acceptance publishes all references/events/receipt after mandatory bytes are durable. Any failure leaves the existing namespace and source archive unchanged. Durable staging inventory records incomplete attempts; restart resumes only validated matching offsets or offers explicit cleanup. Orphaned content-addressed files are collected only after the ordinary zero-root recheck; no failed import can delete a shared object.

Purpose-specific validation: original image imports require static PNG/JPEG/WebP measured signatures and dimensions; masks require static PNG with reviewed grayscale/luminance, polarity and alpha mapping, explicit reviewed resize/mapping for the accepted 25MP/8192-side document envelope, and tiled decode reservations before allocation; originals are retained. Reject animation and unsupported formats exactly as UX §3; no first-frame extraction. A valid larger original is not silently resized or rejected solely by a W1/W2 encoded-byte fixture. Training originals keep 1–100/800MiB aggregate, prepared ZIP ≤201MiB and captions ≤4KiB UTF-8 independently; 1024-square/2MiB per prepared image remain WT fixtures only. Caption app parsing uses WJ depth16/token50000/256KiB authored limits; larger returned provenance streams opaque, not discarded. Font files use R35 exact identity/license/parser caps; tensor/config uploads use the Q08 nonexecuting inspection profile and WA reservations, not an invented 1GiB hard tensor cap. Bundles use PF-1/R39 and may exceed4GiB. Each unsupported or over-budget parse preserves original/staged bytes and explains recovery; it cannot register a usable asset or silently truncate. Raw archive upload and training ZIP construction are different operations; arbitrary prepared training ZIP import remains excluded.

### 20.5 Exact transport evidence and portable provenance TP-1

```ts
type BackendTransportEvidence = {
  class: "backend-transport"; attemptId: string; recordId: string;
  direction: "request" | "response"; exactBodyRef: BlobRef;
  completeness: "complete" | "partial"; receivedBytes: string;
  sanitizedHeaderRef: BlobRef; policyRef: BlobRef;
  access: "backend-only"; export: "never";
};
type PortableProviderRecord = {
  class: "portable-provider"; attemptId: string; endpoint: string;
  requestId: string | null; status: string; assetHashes: readonly string[];
  requestedPromptRef: BlobRef | null; submittedPromptRef: BlobRef | null;
  returnedPromptRef: BlobRef | null; seedText: string | null;
  safeTimingsRef: BlobRef | null; privacyPolicyRef: BlobRef;
  derivation: { profile: "TP-1"; sourceBodyHash: string; complete: boolean };
};
type AppliedPrivacyPolicy = {
  profileId: string; profileVersion: number; evidenceDigest: string;
  requestedStoreIO: "0"; requestedAccess: "most-private-compatible";
  appliedLifecycleSeconds: number | null; appliedACL: string | null;
  enforcement: "documented" | "observed" | "unknown";
  fallbackAcknowledgementId: string | null;
};
```

Exact request/response body bytes live in a separately permissioned backend store, never a domain BlobRef reachable by generic /assets, portable closure, UI projection or ordinary log. Headers are allowlisted/sanitized at ingestion: never persist FAL_KEY, Authorization or Cookie even in transport evidence. Exact bodies can contain signed URLs or echoed input, so access is backend transport/recovery only; no browser download route. Persist incomplete state honestly. Retain evidence with its attempt until explicit whole-document/root deletion releases it; bounded diagnostic copies remain under existing rings. TP-1 exactness refers to received body bytes only, not a full raw HTTP transcript or redacted body relabelled exact.

PortableProviderRecord is a separately derived allowlisted record. Preserve required seed, status, hashes and safe finite timing values; do not copy arbitrary provider URLs, headers, error messages or unknown JSON properties. Unknown fields remain backend evidence unless separately typed/sanitized. Safe asset lineage uses hashes and route IDs, not full origin/path if it carries secrets. Export traversal follows only portable-class edges and explicitly excludes backend-transport, staging URL maps, credentials, cookies, pairing/session state and live outbox authority; it cannot treat every provenance BlobRef as portable. The source body hash is evidence metadata, not a traversable export edge.

Requested/submitted/returned prompt records remain their separately exact UTF-8 blobs, including escaped/oversized content; do not redact authored text or truncate it and still call it exact. They are document content under the existing full-history policy, not copied HTTP credentials. The writer never inserts provider transport secrets into prompt fields. A provider response containing a known credential/cookie/signed-URL sentinel in a prompt is quarantined; complete copy is blocked with a disclosure until the user creates an explicitly redacted, labelled recovery copy. Do not silently alter the exact original or export transport secrets to satisfy completeness. Consumer fixtures distinguish arbitrary user-authored URL text from known transport credentials. Redacted recovery copies carry incomplete/sanitized status and do not claim exact prompt closure.

### 20.6 Explicit deletion and root ownership DR-1

DeleteCandidate is a dependency-reviewed visibility tombstone. Even a never-adopted, otherwise unprotected result remains strongly byte-rooted by its job result provenance. This intentionally frees no candidate image bytes; set-aside/hide is not disk cleanup. Adopted/history/shared roots remain protected. Candidate metadata records the tombstone and retained byte reason. Job-associated hidden result bytes remain in full-history copy closure because provenance still roots them; the hidden row need not appear as a normal candidate on reopen. This is the user's keep-bytes decision, not the audit's alternative root-release recommendation.

Whole-document deletion is the storage-reclaim path, with PreviewDocumentDeletion then DeleteDocument actions through §5.2. Preview yields a durable plan ID/hash, document revision/root-generation, exclusive releasable bytes, shared/retained roots, affected histories/checkpoints/drafts/jobs, external backups and uncertainty warning. Confirmation names the plan and expected versions; changed roots invalidate the plan with STALE_REVISION and require a new preview. Save copy is offered first but not silently performed; external saved copies/backups are never erased. Deletion cannot be undone as a normal edit; reopening a verified copy creates a new namespace.

| Root class | Atomic DeleteDocument disposition | Physical outcome |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Document/layers, all retained branches/undo-redo, document-scoped named checkpoints, drafts/UI and document candidates/jobs/source/result provenance | Remove this document's owning edges in one writer transaction; append workspace DocumentDeleted tombstone + receipt outside deleted namespace | Exclusive bytes become GC-eligible; retained hash/ID/count receipt holds no strong byte edge |
| Shared objects/another document, independent dataset/adapter library, explicit external pins | Keep every independent root; deletion preview names them | Shared bytes retained, never counted as freed |
| Safe-unstarted queued job | Remove dispatch authority/outbox and cancel local stage work atomically before releasing roots | No provider submit after tombstone |
| Dispatching/running/uncertain job | Preview requires explicit acknowledgement; detach minimal reconciliation tombstone with attempt/request identity and nonportable transport control, keep conservative remote slot/charge reservation | No new paid submission, adoption, candidate publication or result download; known-ID status/cancel reconciliation may continue; uncertainty stays visible |
| In-flight import/prepare/copy or active transfer | Writer epoch/root-generation fencing cancels publication, aborts transfer and pins in-use objects until worker acknowledges stop | GC cannot race a reader/writer; bytes show pending cleanup; an already prepared independent saved bundle remains |
| Staging, quarantine, backups and external/provider copies | Inventory by ownership; release document-only staging after worker stop; keep independent migration/backup roots until their own explicit cleanup | Local deletion never proves remote erasure or deletes a user-chosen external copy |

Accept only after the tombstone, edge release, receipt and dispatch fences commit atomically. Receipt reports accepted deletion and estimated eligible/shared/pending bytes; a subsequent GC receipt reports actual reclaimed bytes. No instant or guaranteed byte-freed claim. GC marks a generation, quarantines zero-root objects with a durable marker, waits until no pre-generation IO lease remains, then rechecks references under writer lock immediately before unlink. A new pin rescues quarantine atomically. Restart completes only already committed tombstones/GC intents, inventories partial unlink and continues idempotently; it never infers deletion from a missing view or temporary file. Repeated DeleteDocument with the same command returns the existing receipt. Late callbacks check tombstone/epoch first, retain only minimal reconciliation metadata and cannot recreate the document or its candidate roots. Unknown remote work may keep a slot occupied; explicit risk override remains §7, and does not mean refund, stop or remote deletion.

### 20.7 Optional request cap and uncertain-charge ledger SG-1

Off by default. SetSpendGuard action sets an optional positive integer paid-request cap on a durable spendSessionId. This is a user-visible work session, independent of browser/pairing sessions, tabs and server restart. A new session requires explicit StartSpendSession review; automatic login/restart cannot reset counters. No billing/account management or guaranteed currency limit is introduced.

Before AttemptDispatching, the writer atomically checks the active cap and reserves one count for that attempt alongside the existing concurrency slot/outbox fence. Inference or training request counts once irrespective of output batch size; an explicit new attempt counts again. Safe-unstarted cancellation releases its reservation; once dispatching is durable, uncertainty conservatively holds/consumes the count even if the crash may have preceded network bytes. Known completion/failure/cancellation never proves a zero charge and does not replenish that session's dispatched count. Read/status/cancel/result retrieval consumes no new paid-request count. Concurrent tabs cannot pass the same last slot. If capped, keep jobs local and show paused-spend-cap; no hidden retry. Explicit cap raise/disable or new session records a config event and resumes eligible work; old unresolved attempts remain in the visible ledger and preserve their concurrency holds unless separately risk-overridden.

Ledger rows show attempt, operation, dispatch status, estimate/source/assumptions, charge observation if supplied, and uncertain-charge state/reason. Unknown actual cost is never zero. U07 estimates remain estimates; U08 stays one active provider request. PERF R24/R26/R30/R31 include cap checks, ledger persistence and paints; no new campaign or numeric performance ceiling is added.

